      "rationale": "[Literature audit 2026-11-05 (deliverome-analysis PR 236), verdict confirmed_shifted: insert_after_residue 353 -> 337 on human canonical P56975 (paper numbering: ortholog:mouse, construct species: mouse). Paper (PMID 35579602): \"carboxyl-terminal to L 362 [no deletion], P 358 [deltaTDHL], S 353 [deltaILSDP], or P 349 [deltaKTDS]) ... superscript numbers relative to full-length mouse proNRG3; GenBank accession number: NP_032760.1\". Audit note: Mouse proNRG3 construct; the paper's numbers match neither NP_032760.1 nor UniProt O35181 (both 713 aa; residue 349 is Gly, 353 Met, 358 Val). The deletion motifs KTDS/ILSDP/TDHL pin the sites at mouse P335/S339/P344 = human P333/S337/P342 within the identical PKTDSILSDPTDHLGIEF stretch, i.e. paper numbering is +16 vs human canonical. Results text swaps the deletions (deltaILSDP at P349) but the legend and sequence agree. This site (LA143-NRG3 deltaILSDP, 'between proNRG3 residues I354 and P358') deleted ILSDP (human 338-342) and is the only variant with light-regulated BACE1 cleavage and TGN release; the row's claim of no flanking deletion is wrong. PM fluorescence was measured but no non-permeabilized surface labeling.] L5 names mouse S353 as one of four LOV2 insertion sites in full-length membrane-anchored mouse NRG3; uniquely among the four, S353 required no flanking deletion, making it the cleanest tolerated insertion. Human NRG3 position 353 is also S (confirmed by reading seq[352]='S'), and the position lies 8 residues from TM2 (which starts at 361), well within the >=3-residue safety margin. Mouse NRG3 is a close ortholog; the evidence is therefore indirect/inferred for the human protein. Full-length mouse proNRG3 (NP_032760.1) with GFP at N-terminus and mCherry at C-terminus was used as backbone; the LOV2/J\u03b1 domain from A. sativa phototropin 1 was inserted at mouse S353 (no flanking deletion at this site, unlike P358 and P349) between the EGF-L domain and the BACE1 cleavage site. Constructs were expressed in cultured hippocampal neurons for photoactivation experiments.",
