      "rationale": "[Literature audit 2026-11-05 (deliverome-analysis PR 236), verdict confirmed_shifted: insert_after_residue NULL -> 275: the paper pins the junction on human canonical P56975 (paper numbering: ortholog:mouse, construct species: mouse). validation_level surface_and_function -> detected_only (paper reports surface: not_reported, function: not_reported). Paper (PMID 33320083): \"To identify the subcellular localization of neuregulins, constructs harbored a human influenza hemagglutinin (HA) epitope tag upstream of the EGF-like domain (exon 2). It has been previously reported that this tag insertion site in neuregulin loci does not alter their function (Wang et al., 2001). The HA tag was inserted between amino acids 222 (Leucine, L) and 223 (Serine, S) for Nrg1 protein, and between amino acids 277 (Histidine, H) and 278 (Threonine, T) for Nrg3 protein.\". Audit note: Recoverable: the Methods name the junction H277|T278, which is mouse Nrg3 (O35181: TTPETSTSPKFH277|T278TTYSTERSEHF; human 277/278 are T/T). Human P56975 has the identical context at H275|T276, offset -2, so insert_after = 275, upstream of the EGF-like domain (first Cys 290). Mouse Nrg3 in pSyn plasmids electroporated in utero. Surface: not shown (immunohistochemistry on fixed, permeabilized sections for subcellular localization). Function of the tagged construct: not measured here, cited from Wang et al. 2001; the row's surface_and_function is not supported by this paper.] L7 and L8 (same PMC article) report HA-tagged full-length Nrg3 with the tag placed upstream of the EGF-like domain (exon 2) and explicitly state this insertion does not alter neuregulin function. The EGF-L domain in human NRG3 begins at C290 (first of six EGF-like cysteines at 290, 298, 304, 317, 319, 328); 'upstream' therefore places the tag somewhere in the extracellular region 87\u2013289. No specific residue is named in any ledger line, so the site is reported at region level with insert_after_residue null. Full-length Nrg3 constructs (including mouse Nrg3) were tagged with an HA epitope upstream of the EGF-like domain (exon 2) and expressed to identify subcellular localization of the membrane-anchored protein; L8 (same paper) states this insertion site does not alter neuregulin function, citing Wang et al. 2001.",
